tern emerging in the input rows.\n\n\n**Exploring the Dimensions**\n\nI'm now focused on why rows 1 through 4 of the new input are identical and suspect vertical stretching. I've broken the structure down into A, B, and C sections (top vertex, middle sides, and side vertices, respectively). This approach is leading me to hypothesize two full diamonds. I'm exploring the geometry as it relates to the input and output row lengths, and I'm realizing that the train sets, despite color differences, have identical dimensions, input and output. I believe the answer lies within these dimensions.\n\n\n**Determining the Algorithm**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the core algorithm. The color variations seem irrelevant, which is confirmed by the new test case. The crucial insight is the index pattern, which can be visualized as \"Down, Up, Down, Up,\" like a sawtooth pattern, where the peak number is N-1. I've realized that the total output length, 4N - 3, can be computed for N = 6 rows, so I'm now testing it. The structure is now clear!\n\n\n",
